</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-lagrange-example-circle">
<title>Absolute Extrema on a Circle</title>

<example xml:id="example-lagrange-circle">
<title>Absolute Extrema on a Circle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the absolute maximum and minimum of
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2x + 2y + 5</m>, given the constraint
<m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>

<p>
Completing the squares shows that
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-circle-square" number="yes">
f(x,y) = (x-1)^2 + (y+1)^2 + 3,
</md>
so the graph of <m>f</m> is a paraboloid with vertex at
<m>(1,-1,3)</m>. The constraint can be thought of as
<m>g(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 4 = 0</m>, which is a circle of radius <m>2</m>
centered at the origin in the <m>xy</m>-plane. Restricting <m>f</m> to
this circle traces a curve on the paraboloid, and the goal is to find
the highest and lowest points of that curve. By
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-circle-square"/>, the level curves of <m>f</m>
are the circles <m>(x-1)^2 + (y+1)^2 = c - 3</m> centered at
<m>(1,-1)</m>, and we are looking for the smallest and largest values of
<m>c</m> for which the level curve still meets the constraint circle; as
<xref ref="fig-lagrange-circle-prefigure"/> shows, at those values the
two circles are tangent and the gradients are collinear,
<m>\nabla f = \lambda\,\nabla g</m>.
</p>
<p>
We compute the gradients:
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp= (2x-2)\,\mathbf i + (2y+2)\,\mathbf j</mrow>
<mrow>\nabla g \amp= 2x\,\mathbf i + 2y\,\mathbf j.</mrow>
</md>
The Lagrange multiplier equation gives
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-circle-system">
<mrow>\nabla f \amp= \lambda\,\nabla g</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\;\Rightarrow\; (2x-2)\,\mathbf i + (2y+2)\,\mathbf j = 2\lambda x\,\mathbf i + 2\lambda y\,\mathbf j</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\;\Rightarrow\; x - 1 = \lambda x, \quad y + 1 = \lambda y.</mrow>
</md>
Adding the two equations yields
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-circle-add" number="yes">
x + y = \lambda(x + y).
</md>
This equation is satisfied if <m>\lambda = 1</m> or if
<m>x + y = 0</m>. The case <m>\lambda = 1</m> is impossible, since
substituting it into <m>x - 1 = \lambda x</m> gives <m>-1 = 0</m>.
Hence <m>y = -x</m>, which we substitute into the constraint:
<md xml:id="eq-lagrange-circle-points">
<mrow>x^2 + y^2 = 4 \amp\;\Rightarrow\; 2x^2 = 4
\;\Rightarrow\; x = -y = \pm\sqrt{2}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Therefore the solutions are <m>(x,y) = (\sqrt 2, -\sqrt 2)</m> and
<m>(x,y) = (-\sqrt 2, \sqrt 2)</m>. Using
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-circle-square"/>,
<md>
<mrow>f(\sqrt 2, -\sqrt 2) \amp= 2(\sqrt 2 - 1)^2 + 3 = 9 - 4\sqrt 2</mrow>
<mrow>f(-\sqrt 2, \sqrt 2) \amp= 2(\sqrt 2 + 1)^2 + 3 = 9 + 4\sqrt 2.</mrow>
</md>
Since the circle is closed and bounded and <m>f</m> is continuous, these
are the absolute extrema: <m>f(\sqrt 2, -\sqrt 2) = 9 - 4\sqrt 2</m> is
the absolute minimum and <m>f(-\sqrt 2, \sqrt 2) = 9 + 4\sqrt 2</m> is
the absolute maximum.
</p>
<p>
The answer agrees with the geometry of
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-circle-square"/>: on the constraint circle,
<m>f</m> equals <m>3</m> plus the square of the distance to the point
<m>(1,-1)</m>. The point of the circle farthest from <m>(1,-1)</m> is
<m>(-\sqrt 2, \sqrt 2)</m>, at distance <m>2 + \sqrt 2</m>, giving
<m>f = 3 + (2+\sqrt 2)^2 = 9 + 4\sqrt 2</m>, and the nearest point is
<m>(\sqrt 2, -\sqrt 2)</m>, at distance <m>2 - \sqrt 2</m>, giving
<m>f = 3 + (2-\sqrt 2)^2 = 9 - 4\sqrt 2</m>. Note also that in
<xref ref="fig-lagrange-circle-prefigure"/> the gradients
<m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\nabla g</m> point in the same direction at both
extrema, but with different multipliers: from
<m>x - 1 = \lambda x</m>, we get <m>\lambda = 1 - \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}</m>
at the minimum and <m>\lambda = 1 + \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}</m> at the
maximum.
</p>

<p>
The video below animates this picture. The completed-square form
<xref ref="eq-lagrange-circle-square"/> shows that, with no constraint,
<m>f</m> would take its least possible value <m>3</m> at the vertex
<m>(1,-1)</m>&#8212;the bottom of the paraboloid. But that vertex lies
<em>inside</em> the constraint circle (a distance <m>\sqrt 2 \lt 2</m>
from the origin), so it is not available to us here: the method of
Lagrange multipliers instead locates the highest and lowest points of
<m>f</m> <em>along</em> the circle. As the level circles of <m>f</m>
expand outward from <m>(1,-1)</m>, the first one to reach the constraint
circle gives the constrained minimum <m>9 - 4\sqrt 2</m> and the last
gives the maximum <m>9 + 4\sqrt 2</m>; at each of these contacts the two
circles are tangent and <m>\nabla f</m> is parallel to <m>\nabla g</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lagrange-circle-video">
<caption>
The extrema of <m>f</m> on the constraint circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m>.
The level circles of <m>f</m>, centered at the vertex <m>(1,-1)</m> where
<m>f = 3</m>, expand until they touch the constraint circle; the points
of tangency are the constrained minimum and maximum.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-lagrange-example3"
       source="videos/lagrange_example3_1080p_1.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-lagrange-circle-prefigure">
<caption>
The constraint circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m> and three level curves of
<m>f(x,y) = x^2+y^2-2x+2y+5</m>, which are circles centered at
<m>(1,-1)</m>. The extreme level curves <m>f = 9 \pm 4\sqrt 2</m> are
tangent to the constraint circle, while an intermediate level curve
(dashed) crosses it. At each point of tangency
<m>\nabla f = \lambda\,\nabla g</m>.
</caption>
